        "id": 1139038
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-04-03T14:07:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The signature is completely controlled by the vendor. If they implement this, they will also provide the key which gets send to the phone and passed through the system server as a byte[] to here.\n\nI changed above to \"vendor defined signature\"",
      "parentUuid": "f5526f3e_0a82e4fd",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "c4aea5692571d35b83a2c5fc1dcc3b27d4634e9e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "57407132_9918ee0d",
        "filename": "oemlock/1.0/IOemLock.hal",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000866
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-04-03T16:16:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So, the signature is provided by the vendor and only ever interpreted by the vendor?  Does it only get passed around in the framework as a sort of cookie?\n\nWho does the signature originate with?  From another HAL?",
      "parentUuid": "2ab532c1_40b86b7c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "c4aea5692571d35b83a2c5fc1dcc3b27d4634e9e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "77283823_d24377ea",
        "filename": "oemlock/1.0/IOemLock.hal",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1013030
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-04-03T16:17:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What does \u0027vendor\u0027 mean here though - is it the OEM? Given that this affects carrier interests, wouldn\u0027t the carriers really want to be able to set this?\n\nThe way you write it now, it sounds like every OEM needs to modify system_server to implement a signing algorithm, but it\u0027s unclear over what data or using which key.",
      "parentUuid": "2ab532c1_40b86b7c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "c4aea5692571d35b83a2c5fc1dcc3b27d4634e9e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "78dc1b11_a2429de0",
        "filename": "oemlock/1.0/IOemLock.hal",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1139038
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-04-03T16:35:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The flow would be something like this:\n\n- Carrier decides to allow OEM unlock\n- App on device sends proof of ID to server\n- Server signs a request to allow unlock of phone\n- App passes signature to HAL (system server just forwards the bytes and does nothing to it)\n- HAL implementation checks the signature\n\nSigning is on the server (i.e. not on the phone) and verification is in the HAL implementation. They must share a secret and both are controlled by the vendor.",
      "parentUuid": "77283823_d24377ea",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "c4aea5692571d35b83a2c5fc1dcc3b27d4634e9e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "312218ea_a611f78f",
        "filename": "oemlock/1.0/IOemLock.hal",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1013030
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-04-03T23:22:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ok, as long as the framework doesn\u0027t have to interpret it that is fine. I do wonder if it wouldn\u0027t be nicer to provide some guidance or even a requirement here on how to sign; as it is, the remote backend not only needs to know a shared secret, but it also needs to know how to compute the signature, which can be different on every Android device, depending on the HAL implementation they use.",
      "parentUuid": "78dc1b11_a2429de0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "c4aea5692571d35b83a2c5fc1dcc3b27d4634e9e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e55c28a2_e3bd9fd1",
        "filename": "oemlock/1.0/IOemLock.hal",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1139038
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-04-04T10:29:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The details of this are deliberately left to the carrier-vendor partnership. This is required if they are going to share a secret as that must be kept secret and not well documented. It is also fine to not have any crypto protection and so not requires a signature. The docs say the signature might not be needed but the non-Android parties involved will have to agree on what the process involved will be. Android itself is not involved in the business related aspect of this.",
      "parentUuid": "312218ea_a611f78f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "c4aea5692571d35b83a2c5fc1dcc3b27d4634e9e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b66e5640_082e1c39",
        "filename": "oemlock/1.0/IOemLock.hal",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1013030
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-04-05T16:06:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "But the secret is not the signing algorithm used, right? If you specify which signing algo + what to sign, our partners would only need to exchange keys - that is the secret.\n\nThe current implementation seems to imply that somebody must keep a map of \"device -\u003e IOemLock signing algo + message to sign\".\n\nTo be clear, I\u0027m fine with this going in as-is since it doesn\u0027t really matter for us. I was just curious if there\u0027s any benefit to *not* specifying the algo+message here.",
      "parentUuid": "e55c28a2_e3bd9fd1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "c4aea5692571d35b83a2c5fc1dcc3b27d4634e9e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "60ff6c59_c047e183",
        "filename": "oemlock/1.0/IOemLock.hal",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1139038
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-04-05T16:33:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The main problems I see with mandating an algorithm here are:\n - if a specialised chip is used as the backed of this HAL, it might not support the algorithm\n - if the algorithm needs to change, e.g. for security reasons, we would have to wait until a new HAL version is released\n\nYes, a mapping of device, algo and message is needed. The version query was added to allow for version -\u003e algo rather than device -\u003e algo. device -\u003e message is needed eitherway.",
      "parentUuid": "b66e5640_082e1c39",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "c4aea5692571d35b83a2c5fc1dcc3b27d4634e9e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e3acc6d2_3bfe4073",
        "filename": "oemlock/1.0/IOemLock.hal",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 33,
      "author": {
        "id": 1013030
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-04-06T16:54:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For the first point, you could just add an enum with commonly used algo\u0027s. The second point is a good one; you could pretty easily rev the interface, but you would depend on Android to do it. I would hope we\u0027re usually ahead of the curve of algorithms though.\n\nAll that said, I don\u0027t really like getVersion(); it\u0027s confusing because the HAL itself already has a version (1.0). It seems like the implementation of your getVersion() would somehow be dictated by vendors/carriers, but they appear in the generic interface. It\u0027s not obvious what to return. In that case, I\u0027d much rather leave getVersion() out and leave this generic byte array in place.",
      "parentUuid": "60ff6c59_c047e183",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 33,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 33,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "c4aea5692571d35b83a2c5fc1dcc3b27d4634e9e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
